```c
prog = READ_ONCE(psock->progs.skb_verdict);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `psock` is dereferenced directly from the `strparser` without checking if it is still valid, especially after an ENOMEM error. If the `psock` has been removed while the `strparser` is paused, accessing it can lead to undefined behavior or crashes since it may no longer be valid when accessed outside the RCU read lock context.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
sk = strp->sk;
psock = sk_psock(sk);
if (unlikely(!psock)) {
    kfree_skb(skb);
    goto out;
}
```
Reason:
The fix involves looking up the `psock` again using the socket `sk` after acquiring the RCU read lock. This ensures that the `psock` is valid at the time of access. If the `psock` is not valid, the code safely drops the `skb` instead of proceeding with potentially invalid data, thus preventing undefined behavior or crashes.

